((window.CARDS_BY_LANG = window.CARDS_BY_LANG || {}).en = window.CARDS_BY_LANG.en || {}).place = [{"name":"Eiffel Tower","emoji":"🗼","meta":["France","iron tower","1889"],"description":"A latticework iron tower on the Champ de Mars in Paris, built for the World's Fair as a temporary structure and left standing to become the emblem of France. Three observation decks look out over the city, and every hour after dark the tower bursts into sparkling lights.","fact":"While the tower was going up, a group of Parisian artists and writers published a public protest against it, calling the structure ugly and useless.","wiki_en":"Eiffel Tower"},{"name":"Colosseum","emoji":"🏟️","meta":["Italy","ancient amphitheatre","1st century AD"],"description":"The largest amphitheatre of ancient Rome, where gladiator fights and wild-animal shows were staged in front of tens of thousands of spectators. Earthquakes and centuries of ruin took their toll, yet its arches and vaults remain one of the most recognisable symbols of Italy.","fact":"A maze of underground passages, the hypogeum, survives beneath the arena floor; gladiators and animals waited there before being sent out to fight.","wiki_en":"Colosseum"},{"name":"Statue of Liberty","emoji":"🗽","meta":["United States","statue","1886"],"description":"A colossal statue of a woman holding a torch, standing on an island in New York Harbor and given to the United States by France as a token of friendship. It became a symbol of freedom and hope for the millions of immigrants who arrived in America by sea.","fact":"The statue's outer skin is made of thin copper sheets; over the years they oxidised and gave her that familiar greenish colour.","wiki_en":"Statue of Liberty"},{"name":"Great Wall of China","emoji":"🧱","meta":["China","defensive wall","centuries BC – 17th century"],"description":"A chain of walls, towers and forts running for thousands of kilometres, built and rebuilt by successive Chinese dynasties to hold off raids by nomads from the steppe. It is the largest man-made monument in the world and one of the emblems of China.","fact":"Contrary to a popular myth, the wall cannot be made out with the naked eye from orbit.","wiki_en":"Great Wall of China"},{"name":"Taj Mahal","emoji":"🤍","meta":["India","mausoleum","17th century"],"description":"A mausoleum of white marble in Agra, raised by the emperor Shah Jahan in memory of his beloved wife Mumtaz Mahal. Its symmetrical domes, minarets and inlays of semi-precious stones are considered the high point of Mughal architecture.","fact":"The colour of the Taj Mahal's marble shifts through the day, from pinkish at dawn to golden at sunset.","wiki_en":"Taj Mahal"},{"name":"Machu Picchu","emoji":"🏔️","meta":["Peru","Inca city","15th century"],"description":"An Inca city lost high in the Andes, built from stone blocks fitted together so precisely that no mortar was needed. It stayed unknown to the outside world for a long time and survived in such good shape precisely because the Spanish conquistadors never found it.","fact":"What Machu Picchu was actually for is still argued over: a summer residence for the ruler, or a sanctuary.","wiki_en":"Machu Picchu"},{"name":"Pyramids of Giza","emoji":"🔺","meta":["Egypt","ancient pyramids","around 2500 BC"],"description":"A complex of monumental tombs for the Egyptian pharaohs on the plateau west of Cairo, the best known of them the Great Pyramid of Khufu. For many centuries it remained the tallest structure ever built by human hands.","fact":"The pyramids of Giza are the only one of the seven wonders of the ancient world still standing almost intact.","wiki_en":"Giza pyramid complex"},{"name":"Great Sphinx","emoji":"🦁","meta":["Egypt","statue with a lion's body","around 2500 BC"],"description":"A huge figure with the body of a lion and the head of a man, carved out of a limestone outcrop beside the pyramids of Giza. The face is thought to portray one of the Egyptian pharaohs, though scholars have never agreed on which one.","fact":"The Sphinx has no nose, and one legend blames a cannon shot fired by Napoleon's soldiers, although the damage is older than that story.","wiki_en":"Great Sphinx of Giza"},{"name":"Petra","emoji":"🏜️","meta":["Jordan","city carved into rock","around the 4th century BC"],"description":"The ancient capital of the Nabataean kingdom, its temples and tombs cut straight into the pinkish-red cliffs of the desert. A narrow gorge, the Siq, runs for about a kilometre before opening onto the most famous facade of all, Al-Khazneh.","fact":"Petra is nicknamed the rose city after the colour of the sandstone its facades are carved from.","wiki_en":"Petra"},{"name":"Christ the Redeemer","emoji":"✝️","meta":["Brazil","statue","1931"],"description":"A monumental statue of Jesus Christ with arms spread wide, standing on the summit of Mount Corcovado above Rio de Janeiro. It can be seen from almost anywhere in the city and has become one of the most recognisable symbols of Brazil.","fact":"The mountain is high enough that the statue often ends up inside a cloud, leaving visitors to wait for it to clear.","wiki_en":"Christ the Redeemer (statue)"},{"name":"Chichen Itza","emoji":"🐍","meta":["Mexico","Maya city","around the 9th century"],"description":"One of the largest cities of the Maya, on the Yucatán peninsula, with the stepped pyramid of Kukulkán at its centre. At the spring and autumn equinoxes, light and shadow play across its staircase in the shape of a snake sliding down.","fact":"Clap your hands in front of the Kukulkán pyramid and the echo comes back sounding like the cry of the sacred quetzal bird.","wiki_en":"Chichen Itza"},{"name":"Angkor Wat","emoji":"🛕","meta":["Cambodia","temple complex","12th century"],"description":"The largest religious complex in the world, raised by Khmer rulers first as a Hindu temple and later turned into a Buddhist sanctuary. Its five towers, shaped like lotus buds, even appear on the national flag of Cambodia.","fact":"Angkor Wat is ringed by a moat almost two hundred metres wide, which protected the temple and at the same time kept its foundations stable.","wiki_en":"Angkor Wat"},{"name":"Sagrada Família","emoji":"⛪","meta":["Spain","basilica","since 1882"],"description":"An enormous basilica in Barcelona designed by the architect Antoni Gaudí in a style entirely his own, with towers that look like melted wax or dripped sand. Work began at the end of the 19th century and is still not finished.","fact":"Gaudí used to say that his client — God — was in no hurry, so he never expected to see the building completed in his lifetime.","wiki_en":"Sagrada Família"},{"name":"Big Ben","emoji":"🕰️","meta":["United Kingdom","clock tower","1859"],"description":"The Elizabeth Tower at the Palace of Westminster in London, whose chimes and great bell have long been known to the public simply as Big Ben. The strike of that clock is broadcast on radio and television as a sound emblem of Britain.","fact":"Strictly speaking, Big Ben is the name not of the tower or of the clock, but of the largest bell hanging inside it.","wiki_en":"Big Ben"},{"name":"Tower Bridge","emoji":"🌉","meta":["United Kingdom","bascule bridge","1894"],"description":"A bridge over the Thames next to the Tower of London, its two tall towers linked by walkways high above the water. The middle section lifts to let tall ships through — a rare sight that still draws a crowd of onlookers.","fact":"From a distance Tower Bridge is often mistaken for London Bridge, though the two are completely different structures.","wiki_en":"Tower Bridge"},{"name":"Buckingham Palace","emoji":"👑","meta":["United Kingdom","royal palace","since 1837"],"description":"The official London residence of the British monarchs, where state receptions are held and the Changing of the Guard takes place in the forecourt. When the monarch is inside the palace, the royal standard is raised above the roof.","fact":"The palace has hundreds of rooms, including its own cinema, swimming pool and post office.","wiki_en":"Buckingham Palace"},{"name":"Stonehenge","emoji":"🪨","meta":["United Kingdom","megalithic monument","around 2500 BC"],"description":"A ring of colossal standing stones on Salisbury Plain in England, put up in prehistoric times, long before metal tools reached Britain. What the monument was for — temple, observatory or burial ground — is still disputed.","fact":"Some of the stones at Stonehenge were brought from hills in Wales, many dozens of kilometres from the plain.","wiki_en":"Stonehenge"},{"name":"Louvre","emoji":"🖼️","meta":["France","museum","opened in 1793"],"description":"The largest art museum in the world, housed in a former royal palace in the middle of Paris. Its collection holds Leonardo da Vinci's \"Mona Lisa\" along with thousands of other masterpieces from every era.","fact":"The glass pyramid in the Louvre's courtyard, now a symbol of the museum, only appeared at the end of the 20th century and caused an uproar at first.","wiki_en":"Louvre"},{"name":"Notre-Dame Cathedral","emoji":"🔔","meta":["France","Gothic cathedral","12th–14th centuries"],"description":"The cathedral on the Île de la Cité in the heart of Paris, one of the earliest and most important works of Gothic architecture in Europe. Victor Hugo's novel about the bell-ringer Quasimodo made it famous far beyond France.","fact":"After the fire of 2019 badly damaged the roof and the spire, the cathedral was rebuilt over several years using historical techniques.","wiki_en":"Notre-Dame de Paris"},{"name":"Arc de Triomphe","emoji":"🏛️","meta":["France","triumphal arch","1836"],"description":"A monumental arch on Place Charles de Gaulle in Paris, begun on Napoleon's orders to honour the victories of the French army. Twelve avenues radiate out from it, among them the Champs-Élysées.","fact":"Beneath the arch lies the Tomb of the Unknown Soldier with an eternal flame that has not gone out since the 1920s.","wiki_en":"Arc de Triomphe"},{"name":"Palace of Versailles","emoji":"🪞","meta":["France","royal palace","17th century"],"description":"A vast ensemble of palace and parkland outside Paris, which Louis XIV turned from a hunting lodge into the main residence of the French kings. The Hall of Mirrors and the formal gardens were copied all over Europe.","fact":"It was in the Hall of Mirrors at Versailles that the treaty formally ending the First World War was signed in 1919.","wiki_en":"Palace of Versailles"},{"name":"Mont-Saint-Michel","emoji":"🏝️","meta":["France","island abbey","the Middle Ages"],"description":"A rocky island off the coast of Normandy crowned by a medieval abbey whose spire is visible for many kilometres. At high tide the water cuts the island off from the mainland and turns it into a real fortress.","fact":"The tides at Mont-Saint-Michel are among the biggest in Europe, and the water level can change by the height of a multi-storey building.","wiki_en":"Mont-Saint-Michel"},{"name":"Leaning Tower of Pisa","emoji":"📐","meta":["Italy","bell tower","12th–14th centuries"],"description":"The free-standing bell tower of Pisa cathedral began to tilt while it was still being built, because the ground beneath the foundations was too soft. That very flaw turned an ordinary campanile into one of the best-known landmarks on earth.","fact":"Over the years engineers have tried to halt the lean and even straighten it slightly, using underground counterweights and work on the foundations.","wiki_en":"Leaning Tower of Pisa"},{"name":"Trevi Fountain","emoji":"🪙","meta":["Italy","fountain","1762"],"description":"The biggest and most famous fountain in Rome, crowded with sculptures of sea gods set against a palace facade. Tourists traditionally toss a coin into the water over one shoulder to be sure of coming back to the city one day.","fact":"Thousands of tossed coins are scooped out of the fountain every day, and the money collected goes to charity.","wiki_en":"Trevi Fountain"},{"name":"Pantheon","emoji":"🕳️","meta":["Italy","ancient temple","2nd century AD"],"description":"One of the best-preserved temples of ancient Rome, with a giant dome left open at the centre by a round hole, the oculus. Daylight pours in through it, and when it rains the water runs away along a floor deliberately built with a slope.","fact":"Almost two thousand years on, the Pantheon's dome is still the largest unreinforced concrete dome in the world.","wiki_en":"Pantheon, Rome"},{"name":"St. Peter's Basilica","emoji":"🕊️","meta":["Vatican","basilica","16th–17th centuries"],"description":"The largest Catholic church in the world, standing on the spot where tradition says the apostle Peter was buried. Michelangelo worked on its dome, and the square in front of it is enclosed by Bernini's colonnade.","fact":"The dome of St. Peter's is still one of the tallest in the world, and visitors can climb up to it on foot by a narrow inner staircase.","wiki_en":"St. Peter's Basilica"},{"name":"Pompeii","emoji":"🌋","meta":["Italy","ancient city","destroyed in AD 79"],"description":"A Roman town buried under ash and pumice when Vesuvius erupted, and preserved almost untouched because of it. Excavations have uncovered streets, houses and frescoes that offer a rare look at the everyday life of the ancient Romans.","fact":"Archaeologists fill the cavities left in the hardened ash by the victims' bodies with plaster, producing exact casts of people at the moment of the disaster.","wiki_en":"Pompeii"},{"name":"Parthenon","emoji":"🏺","meta":["Greece","ancient temple","5th century BC"],"description":"The main temple of the Athenian Acropolis, dedicated to the goddess Athena and built at the height of classical Greece. Its proportions and its colonnade set the standard for architecture for thousands of years afterwards.","fact":"Many of the Parthenon's sculptures and friezes are held today in the British Museum in London, a long-standing point of dispute between Greece and Britain.","wiki_en":"Parthenon"},{"name":"Hagia Sophia","emoji":"🕌","meta":["Turkey","church and mosque","6th century"],"description":"A majestic building in Istanbul, put up as a Christian cathedral, used as a mosque for centuries, then as a museum, and now working as a mosque again. Its enormous dome was for a long time the largest in the world.","fact":"Christian mosaics and Islamic calligraphy can still be seen inside, side by side in the same space.","wiki_en":"Hagia Sophia"},{"name":"Blue Mosque","emoji":"🔷","meta":["Turkey","mosque","17th century"],"description":"The mosque of Sultan Ahmed in Istanbul owes its unofficial name to the thousands of blue İznik tiles that line the interior. It was built facing Hagia Sophia and carries on an architectural conversation with it.","fact":"The Blue Mosque is one of very few anywhere that was built with six minarets from the start, something that caused controversy at the time.","wiki_en":"Sultan Ahmed Mosque"},{"name":"Cappadocia","emoji":"🎈","meta":["Turkey","rock landscape","shaped over millions of years"],"description":"A region in central Turkey known for its strange rock formations, the \"fairy chimneys\", as well as for underground cities and cave churches cut into volcanic tuff. These days it is just as famous for hot-air balloon flights at sunrise.","fact":"Whole underground cities survive in Cappadocia, where people hid from raids in multi-level tunnels carved straight into the soft tuff.","wiki_en":"Cappadocia"},{"name":"Pamukkale","emoji":"⚪","meta":["Turkey","limestone terraces","formed over millennia"],"description":"Snow-white travertine terraces in south-western Turkey, formed by mineral hot springs. The name means \"cotton castle\", and that is exactly what these frozen steps of turquoise water look like.","fact":"Next to Pamukkale stand the ruins of the ancient city of Hierapolis, whose inhabitants used the same hot springs for centuries.","wiki_en":"Pamukkale"},{"name":"Burj Khalifa","emoji":"🏙️","meta":["UAE","skyscraper","2010"],"description":"The tallest building in the world, towering over Dubai and visible for dozens of kilometres in clear weather. Its shape, tapering towards the top, was inspired by the silhouette of the desert flower Hymenocallis.","fact":"On the upper floors of the Burj Khalifa you can watch the sunset for a few minutes longer than at the foot of the building.","wiki_en":"Burj Khalifa"},{"name":"Sheikh Zayed Grand Mosque","emoji":"🕌","meta":["UAE","mosque","2007"],"description":"An enormous snow-white mosque in Abu Dhabi with dozens of domes and minarets, faced in marble and laid with one of the largest hand-knotted carpets in the world. It is named after the country's first president and open to visitors of any faith.","fact":"The main prayer hall hangs with giant chandeliers set with Swarovski crystals, among the largest anywhere in the world.","wiki_en":"Sheikh Zayed Grand Mosque"},{"name":"Western Wall","emoji":"🙏","meta":["Israel","surviving wall of the Temple Mount","antiquity"],"description":"The western retaining wall of the Temple Mount in Jerusalem, all that is left of the complex of the Second Temple. For Jews it is the holiest place of prayer, where for centuries people have come to mourn the destruction of the temple.","fact":"It is customary to push notes with prayers and requests addressed to God into the cracks between the stones of the Western Wall.","wiki_en":"Western Wall"},{"name":"Dome of the Rock","emoji":"🟡","meta":["Israel","Islamic shrine","7th century"],"description":"A shrine with a golden dome on the Temple Mount in Jerusalem, one of the oldest surviving monuments of Islamic architecture. Tradition holds that this is where the prophet Muhammad ascended to heaven during his night journey.","fact":"The gold covering of the dome is a relatively recent addition from the 20th century; originally the dome was clad in other materials.","wiki_en":"Dome of the Rock"},{"name":"Dead Sea","emoji":"🧂","meta":["Israel and Jordan","salt lake","natural feature"],"description":"A landlocked lake between Israel and Jordan with one of the highest salt concentrations on Earth; the water is so dense that sinking is almost impossible and the body is pushed back up to the surface. Its shores are the lowest point of dry land on the planet.","fact":"Heavy extraction of water keeps lowering the level of the Dead Sea, and the shoreline retreats a little further every year.","wiki_en":"Dead Sea"},{"name":"Red Square","emoji":"🟥","meta":["Russia","main square of Moscow","15th century"],"description":"The central square of Moscow, right by the Kremlin walls, which for centuries served as a marketplace, a parade ground and the stage for the country's biggest historical events. Its name long predates the Soviet era: the old Russian word for red also meant beautiful.","fact":"The square's historical name has nothing to do with the colour of the Kremlin walls or with Soviet symbolism, contrary to a widespread belief.","wiki_en":"Red Square"},{"name":"Saint Basil's Cathedral","emoji":"🧅","meta":["Russia","cathedral","16th century"],"description":"The Cathedral of the Intercession on Red Square, built on the orders of Ivan the Terrible to mark the capture of Kazan. Its patterned, multicoloured onion domes are like nothing else anywhere and have long been the visual shorthand for Moscow.","fact":"None of the church's nine domes repeats the pattern of another — every single one is painted differently.","wiki_en":"Saint Basil's Cathedral"},{"name":"Moscow Kremlin","emoji":"⭐","meta":["Russia","fortress and seat of power","15th century"],"description":"An ancient fortress in the middle of Moscow with red brick walls and towers, enclosing cathedrals, palaces and the residence of the Russian president. For centuries the Kremlin has remained the working centre of state power.","fact":"The ruby stars on the Kremlin towers only replaced the double-headed eagles in the 1930s.","wiki_en":"Moscow Kremlin"},{"name":"Hermitage Museum","emoji":"🎨","meta":["Russia","museum","1764"],"description":"One of the largest art museums in the world, spread across the Winter Palace and the buildings next to it on the Neva embankment in St Petersburg. Its collection began as the private picture hoard of the empress Catherine the Great.","fact":"A visitor who spent even a minute on each exhibit in the Hermitage would need years to get through the whole collection.","wiki_en":"Hermitage Museum"},{"name":"Peterhof","emoji":"⛲","meta":["Russia","palace and park ensemble","18th century"],"description":"The country residence of the Russian emperors outside St Petersburg, famous for cascades of fountains that run without a single pump, driven only by the natural drop in height. It is often called the Russian Versailles, though Peter the Great meant it to stand on its own.","fact":"Trick fountains are hidden around the park: they soak anyone who steps on a concealed stone or comes a little too close.","wiki_en":"Peterhof Palace"},{"name":"Church of the Savior on Spilled Blood","emoji":"🖌️","meta":["Russia","cathedral","early 20th century"],"description":"An ornate church in St Petersburg raised on the spot where emperor Alexander II was fatally wounded, as a memorial to him. Its Russian-revival facades and domes stand out sharply against the sober architecture of the city centre, and inside it is almost entirely covered in mosaic.","fact":"The mosaics inside cover several thousand square metres, making this one of the largest collections of mosaic in Europe.","wiki_en":"Church of the Savior on Blood"},{"name":"Kizhi","emoji":"🪵","meta":["Russia","museum of wooden architecture","18th century"],"description":"An island on Lake Onega in Karelia, known for its ensemble of wooden churches put together without a single nail. The greatest of them, the Church of the Transfiguration with its twenty-two domes, is the summit of Russian carpentry.","fact":"Legend says the carpenter who built the Church of the Transfiguration threw his axe into the lake, declaring that nobody would ever build another like it.","wiki_en":"Kizhi Pogost"},{"name":"Lake Baikal","emoji":"🌊","meta":["Russia","lake","natural feature"],"description":"The deepest lake on the planet, in the very heart of Siberia, holding an enormous share of the world's supply of fresh water. Its water is so clear that in some places the bottom is visible tens of metres down.","fact":"Baikal is home to the nerpa, one of the world's very few species of seal that lives only in fresh water.","wiki_en":"Lake Baikal"},{"name":"The Motherland Calls","emoji":"⚔️","meta":["Russia","monument","1967"],"description":"A giant statue of a woman raising a sword on Mamayev Kurgan in Volgograd, dedicated to the victory in the Battle of Stalingrad. For a long time it was counted as the tallest statue in the world.","fact":"The monument's sword is so large, and so exposed to the wind, that its structure has been reinforced several times and the material of the blade changed.","wiki_en":"The Motherland Calls"},{"name":"Mount Elbrus","emoji":"⛰️","meta":["Russia","mountain","extinct volcano"],"description":"A twin-peaked mountain in the Caucasus, counted as the highest point in Europe, though geographers still argue over which continent it belongs to. It is an extinct volcano whose slopes are covered in glaciers all year round.","fact":"Elbrus is one of the Seven Summits, the highest mountains of each continent, a set of climbs popular with mountaineers.","wiki_en":"Mount Elbrus"},{"name":"Brandenburg Gate","emoji":"🚪","meta":["Germany","triumphal gate","18th century"],"description":"A monumental gate in the centre of Berlin, topped by the chariot of the goddess of victory, which for decades stood almost exactly on the line between West and East Berlin. Today it stands for German reunification and the peaceful fall of the Wall.","fact":"During the Cold War the gate was effectively closed to passage and became one of the most recognisable images of a divided Germany.","wiki_en":"Brandenburg Gate"},{"name":"Neuschwanstein Castle","emoji":"🏰","meta":["Germany","castle","19th century"],"description":"A fairy-tale castle in the Bavarian Alps, commissioned by the eccentric king Ludwig II as the realisation of his romantic fantasies. This is the castle that inspired Disney's designers when they drew Sleeping Beauty's castle.","fact":"Ludwig II lived in the castle for only a handful of months before his mysterious death, and never saw the building fully finished.","wiki_en":"Neuschwanstein Castle"},{"name":"Cologne Cathedral","emoji":"⛪","meta":["Germany","Gothic cathedral","begun in the 13th century"],"description":"One of the tallest Gothic cathedrals in Europe, its openwork spires looming over the middle of Cologne. Construction dragged on for centuries and was halted for a long stretch before it resumed and was finally completed in the 19th century.","fact":"The cathedral survived the bombing of the Second World War, damaged but standing, while the centre of Cologne around it was almost completely destroyed.","wiki_en":"Cologne Cathedral"},{"name":"Charles Bridge","emoji":"🚶","meta":["Czech Republic","bridge","14th century"],"description":"An old stone bridge over the Vltava in Prague, lined with statues of saints, running from the Old Town up towards Prague Castle. Day and night it remains one of the busiest places to walk in the Czech capital.","fact":"Legend has it that egg yolks were mixed into the mortar of the bridge to make the structure stronger.","wiki_en":"Charles Bridge"},{"name":"Schönbrunn Palace","emoji":"🌳","meta":["Austria","imperial palace","18th century"],"description":"The summer residence of the Austrian Habsburgs in Vienna, with lavish interiors and a formal park that includes one of the oldest zoos in the world. Marie Antoinette spent her childhood here, and the young Mozart played before the empress.","fact":"The zoo in the grounds of Schönbrunn was founded back in the 18th century and is considered the oldest continuously operating zoo in the world.","wiki_en":"Schönbrunn Palace"},{"name":"Alhambra","emoji":"🔶","meta":["Spain","palace and fortress complex","13th–14th centuries"],"description":"A Moorish fortress and palace complex on the hill above Granada, built by the last Muslim dynasty on the Iberian peninsula. Its carved arches, tilework and quiet courtyards with fountains are reckoned the high point of Islamic art in Europe.","fact":"After the fall of Granada in 1492 the Alhambra passed to the Spanish kings, and part of the complex was later rebuilt as a Renaissance palace for Charles V.","wiki_en":"Alhambra"},{"name":"Park Güell","emoji":"🦎","meta":["Spain","park","early 20th century"],"description":"A whimsical park in Barcelona laid out by Antoni Gaudí, with winding benches, mosaic terraces and storybook lodges at the entrance. It was first planned as an exclusive housing estate, but the idea never caught on and the grounds became a public park.","fact":"The mosaic lizard by the main entrance has become one of the most recognisable and most photographed emblems of Barcelona.","wiki_en":"Park Güell"},{"name":"Matterhorn","emoji":"🍫","meta":["Switzerland and Italy","mountain","natural feature"],"description":"One of the most recognisable outlines in the Alps, an almost symmetrical pyramid of a peak on the border between Switzerland and Italy. That very shape is what inspired the logo of Toblerone chocolate.","fact":"The first successful ascent of the Matterhorn in the 19th century ended in tragedy: several members of the party died on the way down.","wiki_en":"Matterhorn"},{"name":"Geirangerfjord","emoji":"⛴️","meta":["Norway","fjord","natural feature"],"description":"One of the most spectacular fjords in Norway, hemmed in by sheer cliffs with waterfalls pouring down them, among them the famous cascade known as the Seven Sisters. In summer huge cruise ships thread their way in between the narrow shores.","fact":"The fjord was carved by moving glaciers, which left behind a deep winding valley that the sea later flooded.","wiki_en":"Geirangerfjord"},{"name":"Cliffs of Moher","emoji":"🐦","meta":["Ireland","sea cliffs","natural feature"],"description":"Sheer cliffs on the west coast of Ireland, dropping into the Atlantic from more than two hundred metres up. In clear weather you can see the Aran Islands and the mountains of the neighbouring counties from the top.","fact":"Thousands of seabirds nest on the ledges of the Cliffs of Moher, including colonies of puffins, small birds with brightly coloured beaks.","wiki_en":"Cliffs of Moher"},{"name":"Edinburgh Castle","emoji":"🛡️","meta":["United Kingdom","fortress","the Middle Ages"],"description":"A medieval fortress on top of an extinct volcano in the middle of Edinburgh, for centuries both a royal residence and a military stronghold. The crown jewels of Scotland are kept here, and a cannon still fires from the walls, once used by locals to set their watches.","fact":"Every day except Sunday a gun fires from the castle at exactly one o'clock — a tradition begun for the benefit of sailors in the harbour.","wiki_en":"Edinburgh Castle"},{"name":"Loch Ness","emoji":"🦕","meta":["United Kingdom","lake","natural feature"],"description":"A long narrow lake in the Scottish Highlands, famous for the legend of a mysterious monster, Nessie, said to live in its dark water. Decades of searches and expeditions have never produced scientific proof that the creature exists.","fact":"Peat makes the water of Loch Ness very dark, which makes any kind of underwater observation extremely difficult.","wiki_en":"Loch Ness"},{"name":"Golden Gate Bridge","emoji":"🌁","meta":["United States","suspension bridge","1937"],"description":"A bright orange suspension bridge across the strait at the entrance to San Francisco Bay, for a long time the longest suspension span in the world. It often disappears into thick fog, which only makes it more recognisable.","fact":"The bridge's colour is officially called international orange, and it was chosen partly because it shows up well in fog.","wiki_en":"Golden Gate Bridge"},{"name":"Hollywood Sign","emoji":"🎬","meta":["United States","sign on a hillside","1923"],"description":"Huge white letters on the hill above Los Angeles, put up in the first place to advertise a housing development and later turned into the emblem of the whole American film industry. Each letter is several storeys tall and visible from many parts of the city.","fact":"The sign originally read HOLLYWOODLAND; the last four letters were only taken down several decades later.","wiki_en":"Hollywood Sign"},{"name":"Grand Canyon","emoji":"🏜️","meta":["United States","canyon","shaped over millions of years"],"description":"An immense gorge cut by the Colorado River into a plateau in the American southwest, exposing layers of rock hundreds of millions of years old. It is so vast that the far rim is sometimes barely visible through the haze.","fact":"The bands of coloured rock on the canyon walls let geologists read the history of the Earth as if turning the pages of a book.","wiki_en":"Grand Canyon"},{"name":"Yellowstone","emoji":"♨️","meta":["United States","national park","1872"],"description":"The first national park in the world, famous for its geysers, hot springs and the supervolcano dozing beneath it. Its best-known geyser, Old Faithful, erupts on a fairly predictable schedule.","fact":"Beneath Yellowstone lies one of the largest active supervolcanoes on the planet, though scientists expect no eruption any time soon.","wiki_en":"Yellowstone National Park"},{"name":"Niagara Falls","emoji":"💦","meta":["United States and Canada","waterfall","natural feature"],"description":"A set of three powerful waterfalls on the border of the United States and Canada, among them the famous Horseshoe, the largest by volume of falling water. At night the falls are lit with coloured floodlights, and in summer sightseeing boats run below them.","fact":"The first person to survive going over Niagara Falls in a barrel was an elderly schoolteacher, early in the 20th century.","wiki_en":"Niagara Falls"},{"name":"Mount Rushmore","emoji":"🪨","meta":["United States","sculpted monument","1941"],"description":"A granite cliff in South Dakota carved with the colossal faces of four American presidents — Washington, Jefferson, Roosevelt and Lincoln. Hundreds of workers spent more than ten years on it, lowered to the faces in cradles hung on cables.","fact":"The plan was originally to show the presidents down to the waist, but the project had to be cut back to the faces alone for lack of money.","wiki_en":"Mount Rushmore"},{"name":"Empire State Building","emoji":"🦍","meta":["United States","skyscraper","1931"],"description":"An art deco skyscraper on Manhattan that held the title of tallest building in the world for decades. Its observation decks on the upper floors are still among the most popular places to look out over New York.","fact":"It was the spire of the Empire State Building that King Kong clung to in the famous film of 1933.","wiki_en":"Empire State Building"},{"name":"Times Square","emoji":"🎇","meta":["United States","square","early 20th century"],"description":"A busy crossroads on Manhattan plastered with giant glowing advertising screens and hemmed in by the theatres of Broadway. Every year thousands of people see in the New Year here, watching the lit-up ball drop.","fact":"The square takes its name from the newspaper The New York Times, whose offices once stood in a building on this spot.","wiki_en":"Times Square"},{"name":"Central Park","emoji":"🍂","meta":["United States","city park","1858"],"description":"A huge green rectangle in the middle of the dense grid of Manhattan, laid out as a place to rest for the residents of a fast-growing city. It has lakes, meadows, a zoo and dozens of spots viewers recognise from Hollywood films.","fact":"Central Park was one of the first large landscape parks to be designed from scratch rather than grown up around an existing natural feature.","wiki_en":"Central Park"},{"name":"Brooklyn Bridge","emoji":"🚴","meta":["United States","suspension bridge","1883"],"description":"One of the oldest suspension bridges in America, linking Manhattan and Brooklyn across the East River. When it opened, its stone neo-Gothic towers and web of steel cables struck people as an engineering marvel.","fact":"Building the bridge took more than ten years and cost the lives of several dozen workers, including the project's own engineer.","wiki_en":"Brooklyn Bridge"},{"name":"White House","emoji":"🦅","meta":["United States","president's residence","1800"],"description":"The official residence and workplace of the US president in Washington, instantly recognisable by its white neoclassical facade. The building was rebuilt more than once after the fire during the War of 1812, but kept its historic look and its name.","fact":"The name White House was only made official at the start of the 20th century, though people had called it that informally long before.","wiki_en":"White House"},{"name":"Las Vegas Strip","emoji":"🎰","meta":["United States","street of casinos and hotels","20th century"],"description":"The famous stretch of boulevard in Las Vegas, lined with giant casino hotels built around themes, from Egyptian pyramids to Venetian canals. At night the street turns into a sea of neon that has become the city's calling card.","fact":"Many of the celebrated hotels on the Strip have been demolished and rebuilt more than once, each time to astonish the public with a bigger attraction.","wiki_en":"Las Vegas Strip"},{"name":"Monument Valley","emoji":"🌵","meta":["United States","plateau of sandstone buttes","shaped over millions of years"],"description":"A desert plateau on the border of Arizona and Utah, known for the silhouettes of giant sandstone buttes worn into shape by wind and water over millions of years. These landscapes are familiar from dozens of westerns and commercials.","fact":"Monument Valley lies within the Navajo reservation and is run by the Navajo themselves, not by the US National Park Service.","wiki_en":"Monument Valley"},{"name":"Alcatraz","emoji":"⛓️","meta":["United States","former island prison","closed in 1963"],"description":"A rocky island in San Francisco Bay that housed a maximum-security federal prison for the most dangerous inmates for decades. The cold currents around the island were believed to make escape all but impossible.","fact":"No officially confirmed escape from Alcatraz ever succeeded, although the fate of several of the runaways was never established.","wiki_en":"Alcatraz Island"},{"name":"CN Tower","emoji":"🗼","meta":["Canada","TV tower","1976"],"description":"A tall telecommunications tower in the middle of Toronto, for a long time reckoned the tallest free-standing structure in the world. One of its upper levels has an observation deck with a glass floor.","fact":"Standing on the glass floor of the observation deck you look straight down at the street far below — many visitors take a while to work up to it.","wiki_en":"CN Tower"},{"name":"Iguazu Falls","emoji":"🦋","meta":["Argentina and Brazil","waterfall system","natural feature"],"description":"A cascade of several hundred separate waterfalls on the border of Argentina and Brazil, stretching for kilometres along the river. The central and most powerful part of the system is called the Devil's Throat.","fact":"The Iguazu Falls are wider and, in a sense, grander than the celebrated Niagara Falls in North America.","wiki_en":"Iguazu Falls"},{"name":"Angel Falls","emoji":"💧","meta":["Venezuela","waterfall","natural feature"],"description":"The tallest uninterrupted waterfall in the world, plunging from the flat top of a table mountain into the Venezuelan jungle. The drop is so great that part of the water turns to mist before it ever reaches the ground.","fact":"The falls are named after the American pilot Jimmie Angel, one of the first to fly over them and tell the world about them.","wiki_en":"Angel Falls"},{"name":"Galápagos Islands","emoji":"🐢","meta":["Ecuador","volcanic archipelago","natural feature"],"description":"A remote archipelago in the Pacific whose unique wildlife — giant tortoises, iguanas and finches — set Charles Darwin thinking along the lines that later became the theory of evolution. Many of the species here live nowhere else on Earth.","fact":"Some of the giant tortoises of the Galápagos live for more than a hundred years and can go weeks without food or water.","wiki_en":"Galápagos Islands"},{"name":"Easter Island","emoji":"🗿","meta":["Chile","island of stone statues","roughly 13th–16th centuries"],"description":"A tiny island in the Pacific, famous for hundreds of colossal stone statues, the moai, carved by its early Polynesian inhabitants. Exactly how figures weighing many tonnes were moved around the island without modern machinery is still unsettled.","fact":"Most of the moai have disproportionately large heads, with their bodies partly buried, which is why they were long thought to be legless.","wiki_en":"Easter Island"},{"name":"Salar de Uyuni","emoji":"🪞","meta":["Bolivia","salt desert","natural feature"],"description":"The largest salt flat in the world, left behind where an ancient lake dried up high in the Bolivian Andes. After rain a thin film of water turns the surface into an enormous mirror that reflects the sky.","fact":"Beneath the blinding white crust of Uyuni lie huge reserves of lithium, a key raw material for modern batteries.","wiki_en":"Salar de Uyuni"},{"name":"Sydney Opera House","emoji":"⛵","meta":["Australia","theatre","1973"],"description":"A concert and theatre complex on the shore of Sydney Harbour whose roof of white curved \"sails\" became the emblem of Australia. That unusual shape made the building far slower and harder to construct than anyone first planned.","fact":"The designer, the Danish architect Jørn Utzon, left Australia after a series of disputes before the work was finished and never saw the building with his own eyes.","wiki_en":"Sydney Opera House"},{"name":"Great Barrier Reef","emoji":"🐠","meta":["Australia","coral reef","natural feature"],"description":"The largest system of coral reefs in the world, running for thousands of kilometres along the north-east coast of Australia. It is so vast that individual structures within it can be picked out even from space.","fact":"Warming oceans and coral bleaching have put much of the reef at risk, making it a symbol of how fragile marine ecosystems are.","wiki_en":"Great Barrier Reef"},{"name":"Uluru","emoji":"🟠","meta":["Australia","monolithic rock","natural feature"],"description":"A giant sandstone rock in the middle of the Australian desert, sacred to the local Anangu people. Depending on the time of day and the weather, its surface shifts in colour from ochre brown to bright red.","fact":"Climbing the rock used to be popular with tourists, but today the ascent of Uluru is banned outright out of respect for Aboriginal traditions.","wiki_en":"Uluru"},{"name":"Mount Fuji","emoji":"🗻","meta":["Japan","volcano","natural feature"],"description":"The symmetrical cone of a dormant volcano, the highest mountain in Japan and one of the country's chief national symbols. Its outline has been drawn countless times in Japanese prints and paintings.","fact":"Mount Fuji counts as an active, if sleeping, volcano — its last known eruption was back at the start of the 18th century.","wiki_en":"Mount Fuji"},{"name":"Fushimi Inari","emoji":"⛩️","meta":["Japan","Shinto shrine","founded in the 8th century"],"description":"A shrine in Kyoto dedicated to Inari, the Shinto deity of rice, famous for the thousands of bright orange torii gates that form tunnels up the hillside. Each gate was donated by a company or a private person hoping for good fortune in business.","fact":"Walking the path through all the gates and shrines on the mountain takes several hours of climbing up and coming back down.","wiki_en":"Fushimi Inari-taisha"},{"name":"Forbidden City","emoji":"🐉","meta":["China","imperial palace complex","15th century"],"description":"An enormous palace complex in the centre of Beijing, for centuries the residence of the Chinese emperors and closed to ordinary people. Today its grounds hold a museum with an extraordinarily rich collection of imperial treasures.","fact":"Legend has it that the Forbidden City has 9,999 rooms — one short of the ten thousand attributed to the palace of the ruler of heaven.","wiki_en":"Forbidden City"},{"name":"Terracotta Army","emoji":"🏺","meta":["China","burial complex","3rd century BC"],"description":"Thousands of life-size clay warriors, horses and chariots, buried next to the tomb of China's first emperor Qin Shi Huang to protect him in the afterlife. Every single figure has its own individual facial features.","fact":"The army was found by chance in 1974 by local farmers digging a well, bringing to light one of the great archaeological discoveries of the 20th century.","wiki_en":"Terracotta Army"},{"name":"Potala Palace","emoji":"☸️","meta":["China","palace and monastery","17th century"],"description":"A majestic palace on a rocky hill in Lhasa, once the winter residence of the Dalai Lamas and a centre of Tibetan Buddhism. Its white and red wings, with thousands of rooms, hold statues, relics and the tombs of former rulers of Tibet.","fact":"The palace stands at more than three and a half thousand metres above sea level, which makes it one of the highest monumental buildings in the world.","wiki_en":"Potala Palace"},{"name":"Ha Long Bay","emoji":"🌊","meta":["Vietnam","bay with karst islands","natural feature"],"description":"A bay in northern Vietnam scattered with thousands of limestone islands and crags of strange shapes rising straight out of emerald water. Visitors are taken between the islands on traditional wooden junks.","fact":"The bay's name means the place where the dragon descends into the sea, from an old Vietnamese legend about a dragon that defended the country.","wiki_en":"Ha Long Bay"},{"name":"Marina Bay Sands","emoji":"🏊","meta":["Singapore","hotel and entertainment complex","2010"],"description":"A complex of three hotel towers in Singapore, topped by a giant boat-shaped platform with a swimming pool on the roof. From up there the whole business centre of the city-state spreads out in front of you.","fact":"The rooftop pool counts as one of the largest and highest swimming pools in the world that are open to hotel guests.","wiki_en":"Marina Bay Sands"},{"name":"Petronas Towers","emoji":"🔗","meta":["Malaysia","twin skyscrapers","1998"],"description":"Two identical steel towers in Kuala Lumpur, for a long time the tallest buildings in the world, joined halfway up by a two-storey sky bridge. Their geometric design draws on Islamic ornament.","fact":"The bridge between the Petronas Towers is not rigidly fixed at both ends — that lets the structure shift slightly as the buildings sway in the wind.","wiki_en":"Petronas Towers"},{"name":"Borobudur","emoji":"🪷","meta":["Indonesia","Buddhist temple","9th century"],"description":"The largest Buddhist temple in the world, on the island of Java, built as a stepped pyramid covered with thousands of reliefs and statues of the Buddha. Pilgrims climb its terraces, symbolically travelling from the earthly world to enlightenment.","fact":"Dozens of bell-shaped stupas stand on the temple's upper terraces, each of them with a statue of the Buddha hidden inside.","wiki_en":"Borobudur"},{"name":"Victoria Falls","emoji":"🌈","meta":["Zambia and Zimbabwe","waterfall","natural feature"],"description":"One of the largest waterfalls in the world, on the Zambezi, where a wall of water crashes into a narrow gorge with a deafening roar. The local name means the smoke that thunders, which is exactly what the columns of spray above the gorge look like.","fact":"The spray from Victoria Falls rises so high that in clear weather it can be seen from several dozen kilometres away.","wiki_en":"Victoria Falls"},{"name":"Kilimanjaro","emoji":"🏔️","meta":["Tanzania","volcanic mountain","natural feature"],"description":"The highest mountain in Africa, a dormant volcano whose snow-covered summit rises straight out of the savanna. Even untrained visitors can get to the top, because the route calls for no technical climbing gear.","fact":"The glaciers on the summit of Kilimanjaro are gradually melting, and scientists fear the mountain's snow cap may disappear within the foreseeable future.","wiki_en":"Mount Kilimanjaro"},{"name":"Serengeti","emoji":"🦓","meta":["Tanzania","national park and savanna","natural feature"],"description":"An endless savanna in East Africa, host each year to one of the biggest animal migrations on the planet: millions of wildebeest and zebra move with the seasonal rains. It also holds the continent's largest populations of lions and other predators.","fact":"The name Serengeti comes from the Maasai language and means roughly the endless plain.","wiki_en":"Serengeti"},{"name":"Table Mountain","emoji":"🌫️","meta":["South Africa","flat-topped mountain","natural feature"],"description":"A mountain with an almost perfectly flat top, rising above Cape Town and visible from nearly anywhere in the city. When clouds spill across that flat summit, locals call the effect the tablecloth.","fact":"You can walk up Table Mountain by several routes, or ride to the top in a few minutes in a cable car with a rotating cabin.","wiki_en":"Table Mountain"},{"name":"Mount Everest","emoji":"🧗","meta":["Nepal and China","mountain","natural feature"],"description":"The highest mountain on Earth, in the Himalayas on the border between Nepal and Tibet. For decades the climb to its summit has remained one of the most prestigious and most dangerous tests for mountaineers from all over the world.","fact":"The first confirmed ascent of Everest was made by Edmund Hillary and Tenzing Norgay in 1953.","wiki_en":"Mount Everest"},{"name":"Yosemite","emoji":"🏔️","meta":["United States","national park","since 1890"],"description":"The granite walls of El Capitan and Half Dome loom over a Californian valley, and one of the tallest waterfalls in North America thunders here from spring through summer. Sequoias, alpine meadows and glacial lakes make Yosemite a byword for American wilderness.","fact":"El Capitan in Yosemite is one of the largest single blocks of granite in the world and a dream for climbers everywhere.","wiki_en":"Yosemite National Park"},{"name":"Zion","emoji":"🏞️","meta":["United States","national park","since 1919"],"description":"Narrow red canyons of the Virgin River cut through the sandstone cliffs of Utah, and the Angels Landing trail, with chains along the drop, is one of the best-known hikes in the country. Mormon settlers named the park, seeing a biblical sanctuary in these rocks.","fact":"Zion's narrowest canyon closes in to a few metres across in places, with walls hundreds of metres high.","wiki_en":"Zion National Park"},{"name":"Bryce Canyon","emoji":"🗿","meta":["United States","national park","since 1928"],"description":"Thousands of orange-red rock spires called hoodoos stand here in an amphitheatre that looks its most dramatic at sunrise. The shapes were produced by centuries of water freezing and thawing in cracks in the limestone.","fact":"Bryce Canyon holds the largest concentration of hoodoo rock formations anywhere in the world.","wiki_en":"Bryce Canyon National Park"},{"name":"Arches National Park","emoji":"🌉","meta":["United States","national park","since 1971"],"description":"In a relatively small area there are more than two thousand natural stone arches, among them the celebrated Delicate Arch. The red sandstone formations were shaped over millions of years by water, wind and swings in temperature.","fact":"Delicate Arch, which appears on Utah's licence plates, is one of the most recognisable natural arches in the world.","wiki_en":"Arches National Park"},{"name":"Death Valley","emoji":"🌡️","meta":["United States","national park","since 1994"],"description":"One of the hottest and driest places on the planet, where in summer the thermometer regularly climbs past 50 degrees. Despite the harsh climate, after the rare spring rains the desert briefly covers itself in a carpet of flowering plants.","fact":"One of the highest air temperatures ever officially measured on Earth was recorded in Death Valley in 1913.","wiki_en":"Death Valley National Park"},{"name":"Sequoia National Park","emoji":"🌲","meta":["United States","national park","since 1890"],"description":"Giant sequoias grow here, among the largest living organisms on the planet, including the tree known as General Sherman. Some of the trees in the park are more than two thousand years old.","fact":"General Sherman in Sequoia National Park is considered the largest living tree on Earth by the volume of its wood.","wiki_en":"Sequoia National Park"},{"name":"Glacier National Park","emoji":"🧊","meta":["United States","national park","since 1910"],"description":"A mountain park on the Canadian border, named after the glaciers that once covered almost all of this ground. The scenic Going-to-the-Sun Road crosses the park over a pass and is counted among the most beautiful drives in America.","fact":"Of the several dozen glaciers that gave the park its name, only a small number survive today.","wiki_en":"Glacier National Park (U.S.)"},{"name":"Everglades","emoji":"🐊","meta":["United States","national park","since 1947"],"description":"The largest subtropical wetland in the United States, a slow-moving river of grass where alligators live alongside rare birds and mangrove thickets. It is the only place in the world where alligators and crocodiles are found together.","fact":"The Everglades are home to the Florida panther, one of the rarest predators in North America, with only a few hundred left in the wild.","wiki_en":"Everglades National Park"},{"name":"Joshua Tree","emoji":"🌵","meta":["United States","national park","since 1994"],"description":"This desert park in California takes its name from the Joshua tree, whose odd silhouettes reminded Mormon settlers of the biblical prophet with his arms raised. Two deserts meet here, the Mojave and the Colorado, each with its own ecosystem.","fact":"Joshua trees, which gave the park its name, are not really trees at all but giant members of the asparagus family.","wiki_en":"Joshua Tree National Park"},{"name":"Crater Lake","emoji":"🌋","meta":["United States","national park","since 1902"],"description":"The deepest lake in the United States fills the caldera of Mount Mazama, a volcano that collapsed thousands of years ago. The water is so clean that it looks a saturated blue, and it is fed only by rain and snow.","fact":"For more than a century a tree trunk has floated upright around Crater Lake; it has been nicknamed the Old Man of the Lake.","wiki_en":"Crater Lake National Park"},{"name":"Mount Rainier","emoji":"🗻","meta":["United States","active volcano","national park since 1899"],"description":"A snow-covered stratovolcano rising above Seattle, reckoned one of the most dangerous active volcanoes in the United States because of the big cities nearby. Glaciers on its slopes feed several rivers in the region.","fact":"Mount Rainier is the highest peak in the Cascade volcanic arc and one of the most heavily glaciated summits in the US outside Alaska.","wiki_en":"Mount Rainier"},{"name":"Antelope Canyon","emoji":"🌀","meta":["United States","slot canyon","popular since the 1990s"],"description":"The winding orange walls of this slot canyon in Arizona have been polished by centuries of sandstone erosion and are lit by shafts of sunlight coming in from above. The site sits on Navajo land and can only be visited with a local guide.","fact":"The canyon was carved over thousands of years by flash floods, which are still a danger to visitors.","wiki_en":"Antelope Canyon"},{"name":"Horseshoe Bend","emoji":"🌊","meta":["United States","bend in the Colorado River canyon","popular since the 2010s"],"description":"Here the Colorado River makes an almost closed loop around a rocky promontory, forming a shape like a horseshoe. From the viewpoint above the drop you look out over one of the most photographed landscapes of the American southwest.","fact":"The horseshoe-shaped bend took millions of years to form, as the Colorado cut down through the sandstone layers of the plateau.","wiki_en":"Horseshoe Bend (Arizona)"},{"name":"Willis Tower","emoji":"🏙️","meta":["United States","skyscraper","built in 1973"],"description":"For decades this was the tallest building in the world, until the skyscrapers of Asia overtook it. An observation deck on the upper floors has glass boxes that stick out beyond the face of the building.","fact":"The glass Skydeck boxes on the upper floors of the Willis Tower let you look straight down through a transparent floor.","wiki_en":"Willis Tower"},{"name":"Space Needle","emoji":"🛸","meta":["United States","observation tower","built in 1962"],"description":"A tower with a disc-shaped top, built in Seattle for the World's Fair and an emblem of the city ever since. Several dozen storeys up there is an observation deck and a revolving restaurant.","fact":"After a renovation, the Space Needle's observation deck gained a revolving glass floor through which you can see the city below.","wiki_en":"Space Needle"},{"name":"Lincoln Memorial","emoji":"🏛️","meta":["United States","memorial","opened in 1922"],"description":"A monumental neoclassical building on the National Mall in Washington holding a giant marble statue of the sixteenth US president. It was on its steps that Martin Luther King gave his famous speech in 1963.","fact":"The seated figure of Lincoln inside the memorial stands about six metres tall and is assembled from many blocks of marble.","wiki_en":"Lincoln Memorial"},{"name":"Washington Monument","emoji":"🗼","meta":["United States","obelisk","completed in 1884"],"description":"A marble obelisk on the National Mall in Washington, raised in memory of the first US president. For a few years after it was finished it was the tallest structure in the world, until the Eiffel Tower passed it; the work dragged on for decades and stalled for lack of money.","fact":"Because of the long pause in the building work, the lower and upper parts of the obelisk are made of marble in slightly different shades.","wiki_en":"Washington Monument"},{"name":"US Capitol","emoji":"🏛️","meta":["United States","parliament building","construction began in 1793"],"description":"The seat of the US Congress, crowned by its familiar white dome and standing on the hill that came to be called Capitol Hill because of it. The first stone was laid at the end of the 18th century, and the present dome was only finished during the Civil War.","fact":"Work on the Capitol dome carried on even through the Civil War, as a symbol that the country was still one.","wiki_en":"United States Capitol"},{"name":"Hoover Dam","emoji":"🌊","meta":["United States","dam","built in the 1930s"],"description":"One of the largest concrete dams of its day blocked the Colorado River on the border of Nevada and Arizona, creating Lake Mead. Building it became a symbol of American engineering at the height of the Great Depression.","fact":"When it was built, the Hoover Dam was the tallest dam in the world, and its turbines still supply electricity to several states.","wiki_en":"Hoover Dam"},{"name":"Smithsonian Institution","emoji":"🏛️","meta":["United States","museum and research complex","founded in 1846"],"description":"The largest museum and research complex in the world brings together dozens of museums and a zoo in Washington, most of them free to enter. It was founded with money left by a British scientist who never set foot in the United States.","fact":"The institution's collections hold tens of millions of objects, from the Wright brothers' aircraft to the famous Hope diamond.","wiki_en":"Smithsonian Institution"},{"name":"Pike Place Market","emoji":"🐟","meta":["United States","market","opened in 1907"],"description":"One of the oldest continuously running farmers' markets in the United States, in Seattle, famous for fishmongers who toss salmon over the counter for the crowd. The very first Starbucks coffee shop opened right next door.","fact":"The market appeared in 1907, when townspeople demanded to buy produce straight from the farmers, cutting out the middlemen.","wiki_en":"Pike Place Market"},{"name":"Disney World","emoji":"🏰","meta":["United States","theme park","opened in 1971"],"description":"The largest theme park resort in the world by area sprawls across Florida and includes several themed parks, hotels and golf courses. Cinderella's castle at its centre is one of the most recognisable outlines in the entertainment business.","fact":"The grounds of Disney World in Florida are comparable in area to a small town and larger than Manhattan.","wiki_en":"Walt Disney World"},{"name":"Meteor Crater","emoji":"☄️","meta":["United States","meteorite crater","around 50,000 years ago"],"description":"One of the best-preserved meteorite craters on Earth was made in the Arizona desert by the impact of a small metallic asteroid. Astronauts of the Apollo programme trained here before flying to the Moon.","fact":"For a long time the hollow was thought to come from volcanic activity, and its meteorite origin was only accepted in the 20th century.","wiki_en":"Meteor Crater"},{"name":"The Alamo","emoji":"🪖","meta":["United States","mission fortress","founded in the 18th century"],"description":"A former Catholic mission in San Antonio, Texas, became the site of a famous siege during the Texas war of independence from Mexico. The phrase \"Remember the Alamo!\" still stands in American culture for holding firm in the face of defeat.","fact":"Almost all the defenders of the Alamo died in the siege of 1836, but their resistance became a rallying symbol for the Texans who fought on.","wiki_en":"Alamo Mission in San Antonio"},{"name":"Denali","emoji":"🏔️","meta":["United States","highest mountain in North America","national park since 1917"],"description":"This snow-covered summit in Alaska is the highest point in North America, drawing climbers from all over the world with the harsh conditions of the ascent. The name Denali means the high one in the language of the native Athabaskans.","fact":"The rise from base to summit at Denali is among the greatest in the world — greater than at Everest, which stands on a high plateau.","wiki_en":"Denali"},{"name":"Saint Isaac's Cathedral","emoji":"⛪","meta":["Russia","cathedral","built in the mid-19th century"],"description":"One of the largest domed cathedrals in the world took almost four decades to build in St Petersburg and became the architectural calling card of the city. The colonnade below the dome gives a panoramic view over the historic centre.","fact":"The monolithic granite columns of the porticoes weigh around a hundred tonnes each, and they were put up before the walls of the cathedral went in.","wiki_en":"Saint Isaac's Cathedral"},{"name":"Kazan Cathedral","emoji":"⛪","meta":["Russia","cathedral","built in the early 19th century"],"description":"The cathedral on Nevsky Prospect in St Petersburg was modelled on St Peter's in Rome and is fronted by a semicircular colonnade. Field Marshal Mikhail Kutuzov was buried here after the war against Napoleon in 1812.","fact":"The cathedral is named after the Kazan icon of the Mother of God, whose venerated copy was kept here as its chief holy relic.","wiki_en":"Kazan Cathedral, Saint Petersburg"},{"name":"Cathedral of Christ the Saviour","emoji":"⛪","meta":["Russia","cathedral","rebuilt in the 1990s"],"description":"The largest Orthodox church in Russia, in Moscow, was blown up in Soviet times and built again from scratch at the end of the 20th century, following the design of the lost original. The first church was raised in memory of the victory over Napoleon in 1812.","fact":"For several decades an open-air swimming pool called Moskva operated on the site of the demolished church, in use even in winter.","wiki_en":"Cathedral of Christ the Saviour"},{"name":"Novodevichy Convent","emoji":"⛪","meta":["Russia","convent","founded in the 16th century"],"description":"One of the best-known convents in Moscow was founded to mark the return of Smolensk to Russian lands. Its grounds hold a cemetery where many prominent figures of Russian culture and history are buried.","fact":"The architectural ensemble of the Novodevichy Convent is on the UNESCO World Heritage list.","wiki_en":"Novodevichy Convent"},{"name":"Tretyakov Gallery","emoji":"🖼️","meta":["Russia","art museum","founded in 1856"],"description":"The largest collection of Russian visual art grew out of the private holdings of the Moscow merchant and patron Pavel Tretyakov. Today the gallery keeps everything from early Russian icons to the canvases of the Wanderers and the avant-garde.","fact":"The gallery's collection began with the personal picture collection of the merchant Pavel Tretyakov, who gave it to the city of Moscow.","wiki_en":"Tretyakov Gallery"},{"name":"Russian Museum","emoji":"🎨","meta":["Russia","art museum","founded in 1895"],"description":"The country's first state museum of Russian visual art is in St Petersburg, in the Mikhailovsky Palace. Its collection spans almost the whole history of Russian painting and sculpture, from icons to the avant-garde of the 20th century.","fact":"The Russian Museum was founded by decree of emperor Nicholas II and became Russia's first state museum of national art.","wiki_en":"Russian Museum"},{"name":"VDNKh","emoji":"🎡","meta":["Russia","exhibition complex","opened in 1939"],"description":"This vast exhibition complex in Moscow was originally built to show off the achievements of Soviet agriculture and industry. Today its Stalinist-empire pavilions sit alongside fountains, museums and stretches of parkland.","fact":"The Friendship of Peoples fountain at VDNKh is ringed by gilded sculptures standing for the union republics of the USSR.","wiki_en":"VDNKh","wiki_ru":"Выставка достижений народного хозяйства"},{"name":"Ostankino Tower","emoji":"📡","meta":["Russia","TV tower","built in 1967"],"description":"One of the tallest television towers in the world rises above Moscow and for decades carried broadcasting to the whole region. Several hundred metres up there is an observation deck looking out over the city.","fact":"For a few years after it was built, the Ostankino Tower was the tallest free-standing structure in the world.","wiki_en":"Ostankino Tower"},{"name":"Tsar Cannon","emoji":"⚔️","meta":["Russia","monumental cannon","cast in 1586"],"description":"This giant bronze gun stands inside the Moscow Kremlin and counts as one of the largest artillery barrels by calibre in history. Historians argue over whether the cannon was ever fired at all.","fact":"For all its imposing size, there is no reliable evidence that the Tsar Cannon was ever used in battle.","wiki_en":"Tsar Cannon"},{"name":"Tsar Bell","emoji":"🔔","meta":["Russia","monumental bell","cast in 1735"],"description":"The largest bell in the world was never hoisted into a belfry: a massive piece broke off it during a fire. Today it stands on a plinth in the Moscow Kremlin as a monument to 18th-century bell-founding.","fact":"A piece weighing several tonnes broke off the Tsar Bell before it could ever be raised and used for its purpose.","wiki_en":"Tsar Bell"},{"name":"Mamayev Kurgan","emoji":"🕊️","meta":["Russia","memorial complex","opened in 1967"],"description":"The hill in Volgograd where some of the fiercest fighting of the Second World War took place during the Battle of Stalingrad is now one of the largest war memorials in the world. The statue The Motherland Calls stands at its top.","fact":"When it was unveiled, the statue The Motherland Calls was the tallest sculpture in the world.","wiki_en":"Mamayev Kurgan"},{"name":"Curonian Spit","emoji":"🏖️","meta":["Russia","sand spit","national park since 1987"],"description":"A narrow strip of sand between the Baltic Sea and the Curonian Lagoon runs for almost a hundred kilometres and is divided between Russia and Lithuania. It holds giant dunes and a dancing forest of strangely twisted pine trunks.","fact":"In the dancing forest on the Curonian Spit the pine trunks are bent and twisted for reasons that are still not fully understood.","wiki_en":"Curonian Spit"},{"name":"Valley of Geysers","emoji":"♨️","meta":["Russia","geyser field","discovered in 1941"],"description":"One of the few large concentrations of geysers in the world sits in Kamchatka, in the hard-to-reach canyon of the Geysernaya River. Dozens of hot springs and fountains of steam make this a geothermal phenomenon with few rivals.","fact":"The Valley of Geysers in Kamchatka was found in 1941 by the researcher Tatyana Ustinova, who came upon it by chance during an expedition.","wiki_en":"Valley of Geysers"},{"name":"Lena Pillars","emoji":"🪨","meta":["Russia","rock pillars","national park since 1995"],"description":"Vertical limestone cliffs tens of metres high run for many kilometres along the Lena River in Yakutia. Bones of mammoths and other animals of the ice age turn up in the deposits of these places.","fact":"Well-preserved remains of mammoths and other fossil animals are found regularly around the Lena Pillars.","wiki_en":"Lena Pillars"},{"name":"Putorana Plateau","emoji":"⛰️","meta":["Russia","basalt plateau","nature reserve since 1988"],"description":"This remote plateau in northern Siberia is cut through by deep canyons and hundreds of waterfalls, and its basalt steps look like terraces. Because it is so hard to reach, it remains one of the least visited places in Russia.","fact":"One of the tallest waterfalls in Russia is on the Putorana Plateau, hidden away in a canyon that is hard to get to.","wiki_en":"Putorana Plateau"},{"name":"Solovetsky Islands","emoji":"🏝️","meta":["Russia","archipelago with a monastery","monastery since the 15th century"],"description":"This archipelago in the White Sea is known for an ancient monastery that has been, at different times, a spiritual centre and a place of imprisonment. The harsh climate and the distance shaped a particular way of life here over the centuries.","fact":"In Soviet times one of the first special-purpose prison camps was set up on the grounds of the Solovetsky monastery.","wiki_en":"Solovetsky Islands"},{"name":"Golden Ring of Russia","emoji":"💍","meta":["Russia","tourist route","took shape in the 1960s"],"description":"The name covers several ancient towns around Moscow — Suzdal, Vladimir, Sergiyev Posad and others — which kept their white stone churches and kremlins from before the Mongol invasion. The route was put together as a tourist brand in the Soviet years.","fact":"The term Golden Ring was coined in the 1960s by the journalist Yuri Bychkov, who tied the old towns together into a single tourist route.","wiki_en":"Golden Ring of Russia","wiki_ru":"Золотое кольцо России"},{"name":"Palace Square","emoji":"🏛️","meta":["Russia","square","took shape in the 18th–19th centuries"],"description":"The main square of St Petersburg is enclosed by the Winter Palace and the General Staff building with its triumphal arch, with the Alexander Column rising in the middle. Key events of Russian history played out here, including those of 1917.","fact":"The Alexander Column in the middle of Palace Square is held on its plinth by its own weight alone, with no fixings.","wiki_en":"Palace Square"},{"name":"Palace Bridge","emoji":"🌉","meta":["Russia","bascule bridge","built in 1916"],"description":"One of the most recognisable opening bridges in St Petersburg links the Palace Embankment with Vasilyevsky Island across the Neva. Raising its leaves at night during the shipping season has become a symbol of the city and a ritual for visitors.","fact":"A permanent bridge only appeared on this spot at the start of the 20th century — before that the banks of the Neva were joined by a pontoon bridge on barges.","wiki_en":"Palace Bridge"},{"name":"Valaam","emoji":"🏝️","meta":["Russia","island with a monastery","probably 14th century"],"description":"This archipelago on Lake Ladoga is known for its stark northern landscape and for one of the oldest monasteries in the Russian north. Rocky shores, pine forests and remote hermitages drew pilgrims and painters here for centuries.","fact":"The landscapes of Valaam inspired many Russian painters, among them Ivan Shishkin, who came here as a young man.","wiki_en":"Valaam"},{"name":"Kungur Ice Cave","emoji":"🧊","meta":["Russia","karst cave","open to visitors since 1914"],"description":"One of the largest karst caves in Russia, in the Urals, is famous for its ice grottoes, underground lakes and fantastic frost crystals. Some of its passages are lit artificially, turning the ice into coloured patterns.","fact":"In some grottoes of the Kungur cave the ice survives all year round, even at the height of the Ural summer.","wiki_en":"Kungur Ice Cave"},{"name":"Krasnoyarsk Stolby","emoji":"⛰️","meta":["Russia","rock pillars","nature reserve since 1925"],"description":"Strangely shaped syenite rocks in the taiga outside Krasnoyarsk have long drawn climbers, including the local stolbisty who climb without ropes. The reserve was set up to protect this unusual landscape and the taiga around it.","fact":"The reserve at Stolby is one of the oldest in Russia: these rocks have been under protection for about a hundred years.","wiki_en":"Stolby Nature Reserve"},{"name":"Olkhon","emoji":"🏝️","meta":["Russia","largest island on Lake Baikal","part of a national park since 1986"],"description":"The biggest island on Lake Baikal is treated as the spiritual centre of Buryat shamanism and has one of the highest counts of sunny days in Russia. Shamanka Rock on its shore is one of the most revered natural symbols of the region.","fact":"Shamanka Rock on Olkhon is counted among the nine sacred places of Asia in local shamanic tradition.","wiki_en":"Olkhon"},{"name":"Dombai","emoji":"🎿","meta":["Russia","ski resort","a tourist centre since the 1920s"],"description":"This mountain village in the Caucasus, ringed by glaciers and peaks over four thousand metres high, became one of the first Soviet centres for mountaineering and skiing. Cable cars carry visitors to platforms looking out at Elbrus and the main Caucasus range.","fact":"From the viewpoints above Dombai you can make out Elbrus, the highest summit in Europe, in clear weather.","wiki_en":"Dombai","wiki_ru":"Домбай"},{"name":"Suzdal Kremlin","emoji":"🏰","meta":["Russia","kremlin","founded in the 11th century"],"description":"The oldest part of the town of Suzdal has kept its earthen ramparts and a cathedral with golden doors, a rarity in pre-Mongol architecture. Its small size and the absence of big modern building make Suzdal a kind of open-air museum.","fact":"The Suzdal Kremlin is one of the few in Russia where almost no later buildings block the ancient face of the town.","wiki_en":"Suzdal Kremlin"},{"name":"Tower of London","emoji":"🏰","meta":["United Kingdom","fortress and museum","built in the 11th century"],"description":"The fortress on the Thames was begun by William the Conqueror and has served in its time as a royal residence, a prison and the store for the crown jewels. The black ravens living on the grounds are said to guard the British monarchy.","fact":"Legend has it that if the ravens ever leave the Tower the British monarchy will fall, which is why their wings are clipped.","wiki_en":"Tower of London"},{"name":"Westminster Abbey","emoji":"⛪","meta":["United Kingdom","abbey","Gothic form since the 13th century"],"description":"This Gothic abbey in London has for centuries been the place where British monarchs are crowned and buried, along with many great figures of science and the arts. Prince William and Kate Middleton were married here in 2011.","fact":"Isaac Newton, Charles Darwin and dozens of British monarchs are buried in Westminster Abbey.","wiki_en":"Westminster Abbey"},{"name":"London Eye","emoji":"🎡","meta":["United Kingdom","observation wheel","opened in 2000"],"description":"This giant observation wheel on the south bank of the Thames was put up as a temporary attraction for the millennium, but stayed on as one of London's main sights. A full turn of a capsule takes about half an hour.","fact":"The wheel turns continuously and so slowly that passengers can step into the capsules while it is still moving.","wiki_en":"London Eye"},{"name":"St Paul's Cathedral","emoji":"⛪","meta":["United Kingdom","cathedral","built 1675–1710"],"description":"This domed cathedral in London was built to the design of the architect Christopher Wren after the Great Fire destroyed the earlier building. For a long time its dome remained one of the tallest structures in the city.","fact":"St Paul's came through the bombing of London in the Second World War almost unscathed, which made it a symbol of the city's resilience.","wiki_en":"St Paul's Cathedral"},{"name":"Plitvice Lakes","emoji":"🏞️","meta":["Croatia","system of lakes and waterfalls","national park since 1949"],"description":"Sixteen turquoise lakes are joined to one another by cascades of waterfalls and by wooden walkways that carry the hiking trails. The colour of the water shifts with its mineral content and the amount of algae.","fact":"The lakes are separated by natural travertine barriers that keep growing on their own and slowly change the pattern of the cascades.","wiki_en":"Plitvice Lakes National Park"},{"name":"Lake Como","emoji":"🛥️","meta":["Italy","lake","of glacial origin"],"description":"One of the deepest lakes in Europe, at the foot of the Alps, has long been favoured by European aristocrats and celebrities for the villas along its picturesque shores. Its shape resembles an upside-down letter Y, which makes it easy to recognise.","fact":"The villas on the shores of Como have served as sets for big films more than once, from Star Wars to the James Bond series.","wiki_en":"Lake Como"},{"name":"Red Sea","emoji":"🐠","meta":["between Africa and Asia","sea","forming for about 30 million years"],"description":"One of the most popular diving regions in the world, thanks to warm, clear water and rich coral reefs. The sea was formed by the splitting apart of the African and Arabian tectonic plates.","fact":"The name Red Sea may come from a seasonal bloom of algae that gives the water a reddish tint.","wiki_en":"Red Sea"},{"name":"Northern Lights","emoji":"🌌","meta":["polar regions","natural light display","observed since ancient times"],"description":"The sky glows when charged particles of the solar wind collide with the Earth's atmosphere near the poles. The colour depends on which atmospheric gases are involved in the collision — most often it comes out green or pinkish.","fact":"The green of the northern lights comes from oxygen atoms glowing at a particular altitude in the atmosphere.","wiki_en":"Aurora"},{"name":"Nazca Lines","emoji":"📐","meta":["Peru","geoglyphs","made around 2,000 years ago"],"description":"Giant figures of animals and geometric shapes scratched into a plateau in the Peruvian desert can only be made out whole from the air. What they were for is still argued over by researchers.","fact":"These drawings were only seen whole in the 20th century, once aircraft began flying over the plateau.","wiki_en":"Nazca Lines"},{"name":"Lake Titicaca","emoji":"🛶","meta":["Peru and Bolivia","high-altitude lake","one of the oldest lakes"],"description":"One of the highest navigable lakes in the world lies among the Andes on the border of Peru and Bolivia. People of the Uru still live on its floating islands, woven together out of reeds.","fact":"By volume of water Titicaca is the largest lake in South America, and its shores lie at about four kilometres above sea level.","wiki_en":"Lake Titicaca"},{"name":"Patagonia","emoji":"🏔️","meta":["Argentina and Chile","natural region","shaped over millions of years"],"description":"A vast, thinly populated region in the south of South America, known for glaciers, sharp granite peaks like Fitz Roy and the harsh winds of its steppes. Its national parks draw climbers and trekkers from all over the world.","fact":"The Perito Moreno glacier in Argentine Patagonia is one of the few glaciers in the world that is not retreating and in places even advancing.","wiki_en":"Patagonia"},{"name":"Golden Temple in Amritsar","emoji":"🛕","meta":["India","principal Sikh temple","built at the end of the 16th century"],"description":"The chief holy place of Sikhism stands in the middle of an artificial lake and is faced with gold leaf, which is where its name comes from. The temple feeds tens of thousands of pilgrims and visitors of any faith free of charge every day.","fact":"The temple has four entrances on four sides, a sign that it is open to people of any origin and any faith.","wiki_en":"Golden Temple"},{"name":"Ganges","emoji":"🕉️","meta":["India","sacred river","one of the oldest revered rivers"],"description":"The river rises in the Himalayas and is held sacred in Hinduism; millions of pilgrims perform ritual bathing in its water every year. Ancient cities line its banks, among them Varanasi, one of the oldest continuously inhabited cities in the world.","fact":"Together with the Brahmaputra, the Ganges forms the largest river delta in the world, overgrown with the mangrove forests of the Sundarbans.","wiki_en":"Ganges"},{"name":"Himalayas","emoji":"🏔️","meta":["Asia","mountain range","forming for about 50 million years"],"description":"The largest mountain range on the planet was thrown up by the collision of the Indian and Eurasian tectonic plates, and it is still growing. Most of the eight-thousand-metre peaks on Earth are here, Everest among them.","fact":"All fourteen of the world's summits above eight thousand metres are in the Himalayas and the neighbouring Karakoram.","wiki_en":"Himalayas"},{"name":"Sahara","emoji":"🏜️","meta":["North Africa","largest hot desert","became a desert thousands of years ago"],"description":"The largest hot desert in the world stretches across more than a dozen African countries, though thousands of years ago there was savanna here, with rivers and lakes. Today it holds both endless sand dunes and stony plateaus.","fact":"By day the Sahara can be hotter than 45 degrees, while at night the temperature in the desert can drop almost to zero.","wiki_en":"Sahara"},{"name":"Himeji Castle","emoji":"🏯","meta":["Japan","castle","rebuilt at the start of the 17th century"],"description":"This snow-white castle complex is called the white heron castle for its elegant curved roofs, which look like the wings of a bird. It is one of the few Japanese castles to keep its original wooden buildings rather than a modern concrete replica.","fact":"Himeji is one of the few Japanese castles never destroyed by war or fire in the whole of its history.","wiki_en":"Himeji Castle"},{"name":"Arashiyama Bamboo Grove","emoji":"🎋","meta":["Japan","bamboo grove","popular since the Heian period"],"description":"Dense stands of tall bamboo on the edge of Kyoto make a shaded green corridor, where the stems sway and rustle in the wind above the heads of visitors. The place has inspired Japanese poets and painters for centuries.","fact":"Bamboo stems in the Arashiyama grove can grow by tens of centimetres in a single day.","wiki_en":"Arashiyama"},{"name":"Tokyo Tower","emoji":"🗼","meta":["Japan","TV tower","built in 1958"],"description":"This red and white tower in Tokyo was modelled on the Eiffel Tower but came out taller than its French prototype. For a long time it was the city's main broadcasting mast; today it remains a popular viewing platform and a symbol of Japan's postwar recovery.","fact":"The tower's striped paintwork, which uses an orange shade, is renewed by hand roughly every five years.","wiki_en":"Tokyo Tower"},{"name":"Shibuya Crossing","emoji":"🚦","meta":["Japan","city crossing","known since the mid-20th century"],"description":"One of the busiest pedestrian crossings in the world, in Tokyo, lets hundreds of people walk in every direction at once when all the lights turn green together. The surrounding skyscrapers with their advertising screens sharpen the sense of a futuristic city.","fact":"At peak moments, up to several thousand people can be crossing the road at Shibuya at the same time.","wiki_en":"Shibuya Crossing"},{"name":"Hills of Guilin","emoji":"⛰️","meta":["China","karst mountains","shaped over millions of years"],"description":"The oddly pointed hills along the Li River are made of limestone and have inspired Chinese painters and poets for centuries. The landscapes of this region count as the classic image of traditional Chinese painting.","fact":"The landscape of Guilin appears on the Chinese twenty-yuan banknote.","wiki_en":"Guilin"},{"name":"Gateway of India","emoji":"🏛️","meta":["India","triumphal arch","built in 1924"],"description":"This monumental arch in Mumbai was raised to mark the visit of the British king George V and became the emblem of the city on the shore of the Arabian Sea. Later the last British troops left through the same gateway after India declared independence.","fact":"The arch is built of yellow basalt and concrete, and its design mixes Indian and European architectural motifs.","wiki_en":"Gateway of India"},{"name":"Red Fort","emoji":"🏰","meta":["India","fortress","built in the mid-17th century"],"description":"This massive fortress of red sandstone in Delhi was the main residence of the emperors of the Mughal dynasty. It is from here that the country's prime minister addresses the nation every year on Independence Day.","fact":"The fort takes its name from the red sandstone walls that run for more than two kilometres around it.","wiki_en":"Red Fort"},{"name":"Ephesus","emoji":"🏛️","meta":["Turkey","ancient city","founded in the 1st millennium BC"],"description":"The ruins of one of the largest cities of the ancient world still hold the majestic facade of the Library of Celsus and a huge ancient theatre. Once a port, the city ended up far from the sea as its harbour gradually silted up.","fact":"The Library of Celsus in Ephesus once held thousands of scrolls and counted as one of the largest libraries of the ancient world.","wiki_en":"Ephesus"},{"name":"Valley of the Kings","emoji":"🪦","meta":["Egypt","necropolis of the pharaohs","used from the 16th to the 11th century BC"],"description":"This rocky gorge on the west bank of the Nile was the burial ground for the pharaohs of the New Kingdom, including the famous tomb of Tutankhamun. Most of the tombs were robbed in antiquity, but the painting on their walls has survived.","fact":"The tomb of Tutankhamun in the Valley of the Kings was found almost untouched by robbers — an extreme rarity in Egyptian archaeology.","wiki_en":"Valley of the Kings"},{"name":"Santorini","emoji":"🌅","meta":["Greece","volcanic island","shaped by an eruption"],"description":"This crescent-shaped island in the Aegean took its form after a catastrophic volcanic eruption in the Bronze Age that reshaped the land. Whitewashed houses on the rim of the caldera and sunsets over the sea have made it one of the emblems of Greece.","fact":"The powerful eruption on Santorini in the Bronze Age probably contributed to the decline of the Minoan civilisation on nearby Crete.","wiki_en":"Santorini"},{"name":"Meteora","emoji":"⛰️","meta":["Greece","cliff-top monasteries","monasteries founded in the 14th century"],"description":"Orthodox monasteries were built on the tops of sheer rock pillars in central Greece, and for centuries the monks climbed up to them on rope ladders. The name means suspended in the air, which is exactly how the monasteries look from a distance.","fact":"For a long time the only way into the monasteries of Meteora was in a net on a rope, or up ladders that could be pulled away.","wiki_en":"Meteora"},{"name":"Amazon","emoji":"🐍","meta":["South America","river","shaped over millions of years"],"description":"One of the largest rivers in the world by volume of water flows through the biggest tropical forest on the planet, which carries its name. Its basin is home to a huge share of all the known animal and plant species on Earth.","fact":"The Amazon carries more water into the ocean than the next several largest rivers in the world put together.","wiki_en":"Amazon River"},{"name":"Lake Garda","emoji":"🛥️","meta":["Italy","lake","of glacial origin"],"description":"The largest lake in Italy, at the foot of the Alps, is known for its mild climate and the olive groves and vineyards on its shores. Small towns around the lake, such as Sirmione, have kept their medieval castles and Roman ruins.","fact":"On the Sirmione peninsula on Lake Garda stand the ruins of a Roman villa traditionally linked with the name of the poet Catullus.","wiki_en":"Lake Garda"},{"name":"Bosphorus Bridge","emoji":"🌉","meta":["Turkey","suspension bridge","built in 1973"],"description":"The first permanent bridge across the Bosphorus joined the European and Asian halves of Istanbul and symbolically linked two continents. When it opened, it became possible for the first time to cross the strait by car rather than only by ferry.","fact":"The bridge opened to traffic in 1973, for the fiftieth anniversary of the Turkish Republic.","wiki_en":"Bosphorus Bridge"},{"name":"Blue Lagoon","emoji":"♨️","meta":["Iceland","geothermal spa","opened in 1992"],"description":"A pool of milky blue mineral water that came about as a by-product of the geothermal power station next door. The warm water, rich in silica and salts, draws visitors all year round, even in the depths of the Icelandic winter.","fact":"The water in the lagoon stays at around 38 degrees all year round, whatever the weather outside.","wiki_en":"Blue Lagoon (geothermal spa)"},{"name":"Palm Jumeirah","emoji":"🌴","meta":["UAE","artificial island","built in the 2000s"],"description":"This archipelago shaped like a palm tree was dredged up out of the sea off Dubai and is recognisable even from space thanks to its distinctive outline. Villas, hotels and apartment complexes stand on the trunk and the fronds.","fact":"The island was built up from millions of cubic metres of sand from the bottom of the gulf, sheltered from the waves by an outer crescent breakwater.","wiki_en":"Palm Jumeirah"},{"name":"Disneyland","emoji":"🎢","meta":["United States","theme park","opened in 1955"],"description":"The first Disney park opened in California to Walt Disney's own design and became the model for dozens of similar parks all over the world. The Sleeping Beauty castle at its centre is one of the most recognisable outlines in the entertainment business.","fact":"A plaque at the park entrance promises visitors that they are leaving today behind and entering a world of the past, the future and fantasy.","wiki_en":"Disneyland"},{"name":"University of Oxford","emoji":"🎓","meta":["United Kingdom","university","teaching since the 11th century"],"description":"The oldest university in the English-speaking world is made up of dozens of independent colleges scattered around the historic centre of the town. Its graduates include a long line of British prime ministers and Nobel laureates.","fact":"Oxford is older than the Aztec empire: teaching was going on here before Tenochtitlan was founded.","wiki_en":"University of Oxford"},{"name":"University of Cambridge","emoji":"📚","meta":["United Kingdom","university","founded in 1209"],"description":"The university came about after part of the faculty left Oxford following a quarrel with the townspeople, and in time it became Oxford's chief rival. The colleges along the river Cam, with their green lawns and the tradition of punting, give the town its unmistakable look.","fact":"Isaac Newton and Charles Darwin studied at Cambridge, and dozens of Nobel laureates are connected with the university in all.","wiki_en":"University of Cambridge"},{"name":"Vatican Museums","emoji":"🖼️","meta":["Vatican","museum complex","founded at the start of the 16th century"],"description":"One of the largest museum complexes in the world gathers the collections of the popes built up over centuries, from ancient sculpture to Renaissance painting. The walk through the galleries traditionally ends in the Sistine Chapel with Michelangelo's frescoes.","fact":"The galleries of the museums run for several kilometres, and getting round every room in a single visit is practically impossible.","wiki_en":"Vatican Museums"},{"name":"Sistine Chapel","emoji":"🎨","meta":["Vatican","chapel","built at the end of the 15th century"],"description":"The private chapel of the popes is famous for Michelangelo's ceiling with its scenes of the creation of the world and for the Last Judgement fresco on the altar wall. It is also where the conclave meets to elect a new pope.","fact":"Contrary to a widespread myth, Michelangelo did not paint the ceiling lying on his back but standing on scaffolding with his head tipped up.","wiki_en":"Sistine Chapel"},{"name":"Uffizi Gallery","emoji":"🖼️","meta":["Italy","art museum","opened to the public in 1765"],"description":"One of the oldest art museums in Europe, in Florence, holds one of the richest collections of Italian Renaissance painting, including works by Botticelli and Leonardo da Vinci. The building was put up as administrative offices for the rulers of Tuscany.","fact":"It is the Uffizi that holds Botticelli's \"Birth of Venus\", one of the most recognisable pictures of the Renaissance.","wiki_en":"Uffizi"},{"name":"Rialto Bridge","emoji":"🌉","meta":["Italy","bridge","built at the end of the 16th century"],"description":"The oldest of the bridges over the Grand Canal in Venice was for a long time the only way to cross the canal on foot. Its stone arch, with rows of little shops along the sides, was designed after several earlier wooden bridges collapsed.","fact":"The stone bridge was built to the design of the architect Antonio da Ponte, whose surname literally means of the bridge.","wiki_en":"Rialto Bridge"},{"name":"Grand Canal","emoji":"🚤","meta":["Italy","main canal of Venice","the city's artery since the Middle Ages"],"description":"The main waterway of Venice curves through the whole city in the shape of an upside-down letter S, with lavish palaces from every era standing along its banks. Instead of cars, the canal carries boats, vaporetti and gondolas.","fact":"Only four bridges cross the whole length of the Grand Canal, so Venetians often get to the other bank in traghetto boats.","wiki_en":"Grand Canal (Venice)"},{"name":"Acropolis of Athens","emoji":"🏛️","meta":["Greece","complex of ancient temples","the Parthenon — 5th century BC"],"description":"The rocky hill in the centre of Athens is crowned by the Parthenon, the temple to the goddess Athena that is reckoned the high point of classical Greek architecture. The complex has come through wars, explosions and centuries of erosion, and remains a symbol of ancient civilisation.","fact":"Part of the Parthenon frieze, known as the Elgin Marbles, is held in the British Museum in London rather than in Athens.","wiki_en":"Acropolis of Athens"},{"name":"Delphi","emoji":"🏛️","meta":["Greece","ancient sanctuary","active from the 8th century BC"],"description":"The sanctuary on the slope of Mount Parnassus was regarded by the ancient Greeks as the navel of the world and was home to the famous Delphic oracle. Rulers and generals from all over Greece came here for prophecies before making important decisions.","fact":"According to legend, the prophecies of the Delphic oracle were spoken by the priestess Pythia, who breathed in vapours from a cleft in the rock.","wiki_en":"Delphi"},{"name":"Colossus of Rhodes","emoji":"🗿","meta":["Greece","giant statue","built around 280 BC"],"description":"One of the seven wonders of the ancient world, a huge bronze statue of the sun god Helios, stood at the entrance to the harbour of Rhodes. A few decades after it was built the statue came down in an earthquake and was never raised again.","fact":"Despite the popular engravings, the statue almost certainly did not straddle the harbour entrance — that is an invention of later ages.","wiki_en":"Colossus of Rhodes"},{"name":"Lighthouse of Alexandria","emoji":"🗼","meta":["Egypt","lighthouse","built in the 3rd century BC"],"description":"One of the seven wonders of the ancient world rose on the island of Pharos at the entrance to the harbour of Alexandria and served for centuries as the model for lighthouses all around the Mediterranean. Earthquakes gradually brought it down in the Middle Ages.","fact":"The lighthouse was one of the tallest structures of its day and remained for many centuries among the tallest things built by human hands.","wiki_en":"Lighthouse of Alexandria"},{"name":"Hanging Gardens of Babylon","emoji":"🌿","meta":["Babylon, now Iraq","legendary gardens","probably 6th century BC"],"description":"One of the seven wonders of the ancient world, described by ancient authors as tiered terraces of lush greenery in the middle of arid Mesopotamia. Archaeologists still argue over whether the gardens really existed and exactly where they might have stood.","fact":"Unlike the other wonders of the world, the exact location and even the existence of the Hanging Gardens have never been confirmed archaeologically.","wiki_en":"Hanging Gardens of Babylon"},{"name":"Mausoleum at Halicarnassus","emoji":"🪦","meta":["Turkey","monumental tomb","built in the 4th century BC"],"description":"One of the seven wonders of the ancient world — the monumental tomb of the ruler Mausolus — gave us the word mausoleum for buildings of this kind. It stood for more than fifteen hundred years before earthquakes brought it down.","fact":"Stones from the ruined tomb were later used to build the crusader castle in Bodrum, which still stands on that shore today.","wiki_en":"Mausoleum at Halicarnassus"},{"name":"Statue of Zeus at Olympia","emoji":"🗿","meta":["Greece","giant statue","made in the 5th century BC"],"description":"One of the seven wonders of the ancient world — a colossal statue of the chief Greek god, covered in gold and ivory — stood in the temple at the site of the ancient Olympic Games. The original is lost, and what became of the statue after antiquity is not entirely clear.","fact":"The statue of Zeus at Olympia was made by the sculptor Phidias, who had earlier directed the work on the sculptures of the Parthenon in Athens.","wiki_en":"Statue of Zeus at Olympia"},{"name":"Temple of Artemis at Ephesus","emoji":"🏛️","meta":["Turkey","temple of the goddess Artemis","built in the 6th century BC"],"description":"One of the seven wonders of the ancient world was counted among the largest temples of antiquity and was rebuilt several times after fires and destruction. All that has reached us of this once grand sanctuary is a single re-erected column.","fact":"Tradition says the temple was burned down by a man named Herostratus, purely so that his name would be remembered for ever.","wiki_en":"Temple of Artemis"},{"name":"Windsor Castle","emoji":"🏰","meta":["United Kingdom","royal residence","founded in the 11th century"],"description":"One of the oldest and largest inhabited castles in the world has served as one of the official residences of the British monarchs for almost a thousand years. Queen Elizabeth II is buried here.","fact":"The British royal family took the surname Windsor in 1917, named after this very castle.","wiki_en":"Windsor Castle"},{"name":"Louvre Pyramid","emoji":"🔺","meta":["France","glass pyramid","built in 1989"],"description":"The glass and metal pyramid in the inner courtyard of the Louvre in Paris serves as the museum's main entrance and once caused an uproar with its modern shape next to a historic palace. Today it has become one of the recognisable symbols of Paris.","fact":"The pyramid was designed by the Chinese-American architect I. M. Pei, and it stands a little over twenty metres high.","wiki_en":"Louvre Pyramid"},{"name":"Palace of Westminster","emoji":"🏛️","meta":["United Kingdom","parliament building","present building — 19th century"],"description":"The home of the British parliament on the bank of the Thames was almost entirely rebuilt in neo-Gothic style after a severe fire in the 19th century. The tower with the bell called Big Ben, formally just one part of the building, became one of London's chief symbols.","fact":"The famous Big Ben is the name of the bell in the tower of the Palace of Westminster, not of the tower itself, as people tend to think.","wiki_en":"Palace of Westminster"}];
